{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Your seal, your staff, and your cord.</b> According to the plain sense, one may give a reason why the pledge was specifically these three. This is because his intention was for the sake of harlotry, and he was despising the seal — the sign of the holy covenant that is in his flesh — and he was despising also the staff of rulers that was in his hand, with which he would shepherd and lead, concerning which he was commanded, ״לֹא יַרְבֶּה לּוֹ נָשִׁים״ (Deuteronomy 17:17), for it is inconceivable that the shepherd of Israel should be a shepherd of harlots, and he was despising also the commandment of tzitzit, which saves from forbidden relations, as in that story (Menahot 44a) where a man hired a harlot for four hundred zuz and was saved by means of the commandment of tzitzit — but this one was not saved. And through this, what Scripture told us here is settled, that he gave these three as a pledge — because it comes to explain why he did not remember the sign of the holy covenant, nor the staff of rulers that was in his hand, nor the tzitzit that was on his garments: but rather because he gave all three as a pledge, and they were despised in his eyes, therefore he did not remember the seal of his flesh — for he gave away outright the seal that was in his hand, therefore he had nothing to remind him of the seal in his flesh. And also the staff that was in his hand he gave away outright; and also ״פְּתִילֶךָ״ — that is, the tzitzit — he gave to her; therefore he had nothing that would save him from the harlotry." "And its midrashic sense: since everything that befell the patriarchs was a sign for the children, even though the patriarchs did not intend this, so too in this account it detailed for you matters that occurred in that episode, to inform you of what will befall the children at the end of days. For the issue that emerges from this union is that from it was born the redeemer for ״שֶׂה פְזוּרָה יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Jeremiah 50:17), to send forth from them the he-goat and the kid — the king of Greece and the rest of the kings of the nations — who butted first in their success like this kid, as is found in the Gemara (Shabbat 77b): ״לָמָּה עִזֵּי מַסְגֵּי בְּרֵישָׁא?״ — 'Why do goats butted first?' — and it concludes: like the creation of the world, in which first there was darkness and afterward light. And without doubt this question was posed by way of allusion: why do the nations precede Israel in their success? And it answers: like the creation of the world, in which first darkness served and afterward light, so too the nations who walk in darkness — their 'serving' in this world comes first, and afterward the light of Israel served. And this is not the place to elaborate on this." "This is what it says here: ״מַה תִּתֶּן לִּי״ — 'What will you give me?' What will be the ultimate purpose of this union? And the answer: ״אָנֹכִי אֲשַׁלַּח גְּדִי עִזִּים״ (Genesis 38:17) — that is, the kings of Babylon, the goats of the flock. ״שֶׂה פְזוּרָה יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Jeremiah 50:17) — through the redeemer who will be born from this union. And what is the pledge? In what will they be assured that it will be so? And he sent seal and staff and cord (Genesis 38:25) — an allusion to three things that are held in pledge all the while that Israel is in exile. The first pledge is the seal of the Holy One, blessed be He — that is, the Name of י״הּ, which HaShem sealed upon the tribes: the Reubenite, the Hanochite, the Paluite, as Rashi explained in the portion of Pinhas (Numbers 26:5). And the fact that the ה preceded the י in the word ״הַחֲנוֹכִי״ is because thus is the measure of every seal: in the signet ring of the Holy One, blessed be He, the Name י״הּ is engraved in its order, and after it was sealed upon the tribes, the letters were reversed, in the manner of every seal, as it says (Job 38:14): ״תִּתְהַפֵּךְ כְּחֹמֶר חוֹתָם״ — 'it turns like clay of a seal.' And this seal is held in pledge, for in every place they were exiled, the Shekhinah was with them, and the Name י״הּ — that is, the seal — cannot be in its fullness until the children are redeemed and are seated at the table of their Father in heaven, as it says (Exodus 17:16): ״כִּי יָד עַל כֵּס יָ״הּ״ — 'for a hand is upon the throne of י-ה.' The second pledge is the א of כִּסֵּא ('throne'), as it says ״כִּי יָד עַל כֵּס יָ״הּ״ — for the Name is not whole and the Throne is not whole until the future. And this is ״פְּתִילֶךָ״ — the tzitzit, for tekhelet resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the firmament, and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory (Menahot 43b); if so, the tekhelet in the tzitzit recalls the Throne of Glory. And the cord's being a pledge alludes to the Throne being somewhat held in pledge, and in the future the Name will be whole and the Throne will be whole, as it says (Psalms 132:13): ״אִוָּהּ לְמוֹשָׁב לוֹ״ — for the letters אִוָּה fill out the Name and the Throne." "Another matter: ״פְּתִילֶךָ״ alludes to what is said of them, ״וְקִצֵּץ פְּתִילִים״ (Exodus 39:3), in the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and all of them were held in pledge, as is found in the midrash (Exodus Rabbah 51:3) on ״אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן מִשְׁכַּן״ — that the word מִשְׁכַּן is written twice, signifying that it was taken as a pledge (נִתְמַשְׁכֵּן) twice, because of the sin of Israel, etc. ״וּמַטְּךָ״ — this is the pledge of the ו of אֵלִיָּהוּ, for the shape of the letter ו is like the shape of the staff. And our Sages of blessed memory said (in a midrash on missing and extra letters) that five times אֵלִיָּהוּ is written without a ו, and five times יַעֲקוֹב is written full with a ו, for Elijah left them as a pledge to Jacob — that he would come and redeem his children. And perhaps it is for this reason that he took the ו as a pledge: an allusion to the staff and the scepter of rulers that would be in the hand of Judah, held in pledge all the days of the desolation. Or an allusion to the ו of the Great Name, which is held in pledge, for in this world one makes use only of the Name י״הּ. And there are those who say that Elijah gave him a clap of the hand — that he would redeem them, and the five fingers of the hand resemble five וִי״ן. And all these matters are clear to the one who understands, and the honor of HaShem is to hide a matter; let a wise man hear and add instruction."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {2 "The verse citation from Psalms (אִוָּהּ לְמוֹשָׁב לוֹ) is actually Psalms 132:14 in standard enumeration; the author writes 132:13. Retained his citation as written.", 3 "The phrase 'במדרש חסרות ויתירות' refers to the well-known midrashic work on plene and defective spellings (ספר חסרות ויתירות); rendered descriptively. For וָא״ו in letter-name contexts, read as the letter ו (vav)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:51.483256281Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}